{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1557237-what-is-good-faith-criticism-to-you","id":1557237,"title":"What is good faith criticism to you.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-01-03T12:05:38-08:00","id":1557237,"title":"What is good faith criticism to you.","content":"In an effort to improve my media literacy and give better details on movies, books, and tv shows on what I like or dislike.<br /> <br /> What are examples of good faith criticism. Does it go beyond storytelling, character arcs and the rules of said world building and what is good length for a review.<br /> <br /> I don't want it to short and not give enough details, but I don't want to create a long rambling post that takes forever to get to the point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-03T14:15:54-08:00","id":1557245,"title":"","content":"Since your goal is to improve your media-literacy, I'm gonna address this not in the way you phrased it. <br /> <br /> The medium I'm gonna focus on here will be film as an example. That's far easier than literature, of course. And what I want to outline here is how you get to a place where you feel comfortable critiquing said medium.<ul><li>The first step is to simply gather experience. And for that, you can simply look at <a href=\"https://www.bfi.org.uk/sight-and-sound/greatest-films-all-time\" target=\"_blank\">the Sight And Sound list</a>. Whenever you have time, pick something from that list and watch it. Watch as much of the list as possible. This is a critic-selected list of the best movies of all time.</li> <li>Next you watch as many movies as possible. Just as a suggestion for an exercise: Take your favorite Youtubers who do movie-reviews and they probably did something like a &quot;Best Top 5 movies 2025&quot; and a &quot;Worst Top 5 movies 2025&quot; video. Watch all of the movies they mentioned.</li> <li>Then get a hold of some compendium of good critics (like Roger Ebert or Pauline Kael, for example). The point isn't that you need to agree with everything they write and think. Just get a feel for their style and if any of their writing makes you curious about a movie, watch it.</li> <li>Well, this one is optional if this is just about formulating a critical opinion about something. But there's of course the aspect of learning about the theory and the craft involved in a medium. I don't think it's absolutely necessary to focus on that and if you spend long enough reading stuff about a particular medium, you will pick up things here and there.</li> </ul><br /> Ultimately, the goals are: 1) You need to figure out what you like. Nobody is gonna tell you what that is. You can only find out for yourself. 2) The more experience you have, the more specific you can be in describing what you like and dislike. 3) After knowing what you like and dislike, the challenge is to put those feelings into words and that's why you need to read other critics. To get a feel for the style and also examine differences between how you look at a movie and how the movie-critic in question looked at it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale","name":"Knightingale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27535_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-03T16:23:41-08:00","id":1557247,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the advice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-06T03:14:42-08:00","id":1557338,"title":"","content":"I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27535-knightingale\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar27535_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27535\" data-vbnamecard=\"27535\">Knightingale</a>'s take is good, but I would do differently, because it worked with me.<br /> <br /> One thing about &quot;good faith&quot; when it comes to criticism is to learn the difference between what's a Work Problem and what's a You Problem. When you don't like a work because you disagree with premises or narrative goals, because you think the technique is cheap, because you see lack of quality or creativity, those are valid Work Problems regardless if others will agree with you or not. But if you don't like a given genre and the work is in said genre, we have a big You Problem here. This doesn't mean you can't make a valuable criticism, but it does mean you have to be aware of your bias and remember that bashing the genre for the sake of it won't do you any favors.<br /> <br /> Usually, what we call &quot;bad faith&quot; criticism means hiding your bias, consciously or not. You don't like the work for a reason, but you make up other reasons to criticize it. Learning can help you avoid those traps, but there are plenty professional critics that just got better at flourishing bad faith arguments.<br /> <br /> My suggestion is to pick a few works you like and then search for commentary, criticism and behind-the-scenes stuff to understand what makes it. The idea isn't to agree with whatever someone says, but to look at the elements they point to, consume it again with attention to details you're told about but didn't knew where to look. You can do the same with works you <i>don't </i>like, so you can compare both the things that attract you and the things that repulses you.<br /> <br /> For theory, I think nowadays you can do worse than diving at TV Tropes. It won't just teach you a lot of things, even if not with the most academic view or language, it is a crash course on elements of story-craft you will later be able to recognize and discuss either talking to other people or reading a more serious analysis.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-19T23:07:33-07:00","id":1561961,"title":"","content":"Example of a good faith critiquue.<br /> <br /> &quot;I understand that the showrunners wanted to add some humor, but the jokes don't land all that well.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:55:44-07:00","id":1561963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1557237-what-is-good-faith-criticism-to-you?p=1561961#post1561961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Example of a good faith critiquue.<br /> <br /> &quot;I understand that the showrunners wanted to add some humor, but the jokes don't land all that well.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes and no.<br /> <br /> It's at least a considerate way of putting it, and that's good.<br /> <br /> But is it the real problem? Can you more of less point what didn't work?<br /> <br /> Good faith isn't just the way you phrase it, but how relevant and actionable the point is. Good criticism also don't need just good faith, given your original goals it also needs high quality commentary and details, it needs to answer truthfully &quot;what&quot;, &quot;why&quot;, &quot;how&quot;.<br /> <br /> Also needs to address what works, what is not just acceptable, but a good thing the work did or tried, even in the same aspect.<br /> <br /> One exemple could be:<br /> <br /> &quot;Adding humor was a good call and the themes of the jokes were good, but it didn't work in the end. Half of them happen at bad moments, interrupting the mood of an ongoing scene before its closure, and most of them have bad timing. It's clear the actors aren't comedians, but are trying to.<br /> <br /> The texts themselves aren't great, but not really bad, for a rerun or future season I'd suggest to hire an actual comedian, not so much to write the jokes but to give advice. And definitely take more care on when to place them, the director is good in their environment, don't sacrifice your own pacing sense to add extraneous text.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-20T12:20:24-07:00","id":1561976,"title":"","content":"Good point Montparnasse.<br /> <br /> Here's a example of what I hope is good solid critic of how some shows or movies that have aged badly.<br /> <br /> In Best of Both Worlds from Star Trek Generation where we are introduced to Lt. Commander Shelby where the Borg are making their presence felt. There's a scene where the aged Admiral doesn't really make it clear what he meant when tells Captain Picard why he has a strong interest in Shelby when he makes the statement of musings of a old man's fantasy.<br /> <br /> I can see why people could see it that it seems like that his interest is sexual and not be that is musings of what he was like at her age burning with energy and enthusiasm about their assignments. The writers of the episode should have fine tuned that scene and not make the Admiral seem like a dirty old man.<br /> <br /> To further add it's a valid criticism that some of the women in Star Trek were meant for eye candy because of how The uniform would differ from the men or fellow female cast members.<br /> <br /> You can see this with Marina Surtis, Jeri Ryan and Jolene Blalock from there respective series.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 07-20-2026, 03:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}